  "raw": "{\n  \"audits\": [\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q001\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_h212\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"B: approximately 2075.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"At successive 25-year intervals, the populations are 200, 600, 1800, 5400, and 16200. Thus the population is approximately 6000 after three intervals, or 75 years, placing the estimate near 1998 + 75 = 2073. The best listed estimate is 2075. Recognizing and applying the common growth factor of 3 directly fits Applications of Geometric Sequences; logarithms are unnecessary.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q002\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p111\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"equal-time-equation\",\n          \"label\": \"Equal-time equation\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\"cnm_045\", \"cnm_043\"]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"inverse-page-time-ratio\",\n          \"label\": \"Inverse per-page times\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\"cnm_p214\", \"cnm_p187\"]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"C: page 456.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"If Chandra reads x pages, Bob reads 760 − x pages. Equal reading times give 30x = 45(760 − x), so 75x = 34200 and x = 456. Bob reads 304 pages, and each spends 13680 seconds reading. Alternatively, the page counts are inversely proportional to seconds per page, giving Chandra:Bob = 45:30 = 3:2 and Chandra's share 760 × 3/5 = 456. The assessed quantitative relationships and both optional routes are defensible; neither route is universally required.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q003\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p111\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"common-time-equation\",\n          \"label\": \"Common-time equation\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\"cnm_045\", \"cnm_044\"]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"inverse-page-time-ratio\",\n          \"label\": \"Inverse per-page times\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\"cnm_p214\", \"cnm_p187\"]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"E: 7200 seconds each.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"For a common reading time t seconds, the total page count is t/20 + t/45 + t/30 = 760. Multiplication by 180 gives 9t + 4t + 6t = 136800, hence t = 7200. Alice, Bob, and Chandra read 360, 160, and 240 pages respectively, so whole-page sections are feasible. Alternatively, allocate pages in the inverse per-page-time ratio 9:4:6 and calculate the common time. The common quantitative relationships and both optional routes fit the task.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q004\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_h212\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_p139\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"C: approximately 100 years from 1998.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"The area requirement limits the population to 24900 square miles divided by 1.5 square miles per person, or 16600 people. After four 25-year intervals, the population is 200 × 3^4 = 16200, close to that capacity. Thus the requested estimate is 100 years. Decimal division supports the capacity calculation, while applying repeated tripling is the principal assessed procedure. The estimate does not assert exact equality at 100 years.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q005\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"math:floor-ceiling\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_p111\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"B: 6 sticks of butter.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"The minimum number of full recipes is ceiling(216/15) = 15, since 14 recipes supply only 210 cookies while 15 supply 225. These recipes require 15 × 3 = 45 tablespoons of butter. The minimum whole-stick count is ceiling(45/8) = 6, since five sticks provide only 40 tablespoons and six provide 48. Both upward-rounding steps directly instantiate the definition of ceiling. Formal ceiling notation is optional; the quantitative relationships provide supporting arithmetic.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q006\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p196\", \"math:floor-ceiling\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_p111\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"E: 11 recipes.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"A 25% attendance reduction leaves 108 × 0.75 = 81 students. At two cookies per student, they need 162 cookies. The minimum number of full recipes is ceiling(162/15) = 11: ten recipes provide only 150 cookies, while eleven provide 165. Finding the remaining percentage and selecting the least sufficient integer batch count support both assessed scopes. The cookies-per-student relationship is appropriate supporting scope; explicit ceiling notation is not required.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}",
